text: draw emoji with the host emoji face, and report their ink

An emoji cluster was painted with whatever font was current. That inks only
when the current font is a JDK logical composite. A font created from a FILE
(the bundled Roboto, any app typeface) has no fallback behind it and painted
its .notdef box once per codepoint. Measured on JDK 21 / macOS:
    Roboto(file).canDisplay(U+1F600) = false -> 3 boxes, 0 coloured pixels
In Instagram 455 (its UI face is a file font) every emoji in a bio, a caption
or a comment was a box.

1. Draw. MacApkFont.emojiImage shapes the cluster with the host face
   ("Apple Color Emoji") and Canvas.macapkDrawStringRuns paints that instead.
   - SHAPED (Font.layoutGlyphVector), because drawString does not ligate this
     face: a flag, a skin-toned hand and a keycap each came out as two glyphs
     ~2 em wide (ink 182/193/164 px at 100 px); shaped they are one glyph of
     one em.
   - Through an IMAGE, because a colour glyph is painted only when both the
     colour and the composite are fully opaque: at any other alpha
     drawGlyphVector / drawString paint nothing (0 pixels on INT_ARGB,
     INT_ARGB_PRE and INT_RGB). A 60% secondary label or a fade would lose its
     emoji. The image is cached per (cluster, size, device scale in eighths),
     bounded by count and bytes, and the paint's alpha rides on the composite.
   - Placed with NotoColorEmoji's geometry: art ~1.1 em across whose centre is
     0.34 em above the baseline (136x128 strike at 109 ppem, bearingY 101). The
     host face is set at 1.2x the text size and dropped until the centres
     agree. The cell is still emojiAdvance(), so measureText is unchanged.

2. Clusters. emojiClusterEnd now takes the primary font and also recognises
   - keycaps ([#*0-9] VS16? U+20E3), which start below the old U+203C reject;
   - a text-default base followed by a skin tone (an emoji modifier sequence);
   - a text-default emoji with no variation selector when the emoji family is
     the first in the chain to cover it. fonts.xml lists NotoColorEmoji after
     NotoSansSymbols-Regular-Subsetted and before -Subsetted2, so a bare
     U+2764 is a red heart on a device; here -Subsetted2 won and drew it black.
     Of the 205 text-default emoji >= U+203C: Roboto 3, Subsetted 86 (stay
     text), Subsetted2 12 and uncovered 104 (now emoji).
   The inner loop of a text run breaks where a cluster starts
   (emojiStartsAt), or the digit of a keycap is swallowed by the run before it.
   Paint.macapkMeasureRuns uses the same calls, so measure and draw agree.

3. Ink bounds. A colour glyph has no outline, so getVisualBounds was empty and
   Paint.getTextBounds answered [0,0,0,0] for an emoji. Instagram sizes the
   bitmaps of its quick-reaction emoji from getTextBounds of U+1F601 and got a
   bitmap with no room for the glyph. Now the strike box a device reports:
   [0, -101/109, 136/109, 27/109] em -> [0,-30,40,8] at 32 px.

MACAPK_EMOJI_FACE=0 restores the old draw. MACAPK_TRACE_TEXT=1 (diagnostic,
off by default) logs each distinct non-Latin-1 drawText with the font it
resolved to; it is what showed the remaining boxes were a stale on-disk cache
of the app's own emoji sprites, not a draw.

Verified in the app: bio, caption, comments and the quick-reaction row show
colour emoji; boot 5/5.

